course: dictionary
lesson: 13847
title: The jitters: nerves before something big
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes a word tells you how someone feels inside. «Mia had the jitters before her piano recital, and her hands wobbled.» Before a recital is a clue that something important is coming. Wobbling hands are a clue that she is shaky. So the jitters must be a nervous, shaky feeling.
explain: Jitters is a word for a very common feeling. Almost everyone gets the jitters before something big, like a test, a match or a first day. «I always get the jitters before a spelling test, even when I know my words.» The word is in the plural, so we say the jitters, not a jitter.
explain: Jitters is informal. That means you will hear it in chatty talk, school playgrounds and stories more than in very formal writing. When you meet it, read the words around it: look for a big event, and for signs like shaking or fidgeting, and you will usually find the meaning.
fact: Jitters means a feeling of being very nervous and shaky, especially before something important.
fact: Jitters is nearly always used in the plural: the jitters.
fact: Jitters is informal, so it is common in everyday speech and stories.
fact: Common phrases are get the jitters, have the jitters and pre-match jitters.
fact: Context clues, such as before a match, a test or a show, often tell you the jitters are about nerves.
exercise: «Mia had the jitters before her piano recital, and her hands wobbled.» What are the jitters? | A dance step from the seaside | Being very sleepy after lunch | A tasty pudding made with jam | *A feeling of being very nervous and shaky
exercise: «Ben got the jitters on the first night of the school play.» Which phrase means the same as got the jitters? | Felt very proud of a painting | *Felt very nervous before something big | Felt very hungry at lunchtime | Felt very cold in the snow
exercise: Which words in these sentences are the best clues to what the jitters means? «I always get the jitters before a test.» | get the | *before a test | I always | a test
test: «Before her spelling test, Ella had exam jitters and her pencil shook.» What does exam jitters mean? | Being very good at spelling | *Feeling very nervous before an exam | Wanting to leave school early | Feeling very tired after an exam
test: Which phrase describes nerves before a football match? | Post-match sleep | Pre-match sandwiches | A football made of jelly | *Pre-match jitters
exam: «Noah had the jitters on his first day, but his new friend made him smile.» What does the jitters mean in this sentence? | Being too full after lunch | *Nervous, shaky feelings before something new | Excited shouting in the playground | A cold or a sore throat
